  "summary": "Gears, springs and automata — the Renaissance's mechanical future.",
  "description": "Clockpunk imagines the technological future built on Renaissance engineering: da Vinci-style flying machines, self-moving automata, cathedral-sized orreries, spring-driven arms and gear-punk weapons, all rendered in woodcut, sepia paper and polished brass. The term — a branch of the -punk family coined online in the 2000s — draws on Leonardo's notebooks, the mechanical curiosity cabinets of Rudolf II, and 16th-century automaton masterpieces. Games (Thief's Hammerite machines, Assassin's gear cities), novels and cosplayers use the aesthetic for a future that smells of wood shavings, hot metal and church incense instead of oil.",
  "culturalContext": "Clockpunk gives the Renaissance a future: da Vinci notebooks and Habsburg automata supply the grammar, games (Thief's Hammerites) and cosplay sustain the scene, and the style offers steampunk's warmth without coal-smoke — engineering as humanist craft.",
